WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.410
Pixelmator Photo has just been released for
iPad and I’ve spent some time editing some

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:08.340
RAW and JPEG photos, and overall, I’m really
impressed.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.099
It’s priced at a relatively low $5 on the
App Store which is a good price for the average

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:14.099
user.

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:18.770
We tried it on the 2019 iPad Air and iPad
Mini and it said it was unsupported, due to

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:23.200
the fact that it’s packing the A12 processor
from the iPhone XS, so it must think it’s

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.160
an iPhone instead of an iPad.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:26.570
But it should get fixed soon.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:31.680
Because of that, we tested it on the 2018
iPad Pro using the Apple Pencil 2, and the

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.070
editing experience was excellent.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.500
Importing RAW photos was incredibly easy.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.640
All it took was a cheap USB-C to USB-A adapter
and an SD Card reader.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.860
The photos showed up instantly and the import
took just a couple of seconds.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.610
The UI works great and it’s incredibly simple
to browse and find photos to import.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.969
Importing a RAW photo was very quick, and
the layout is very simple, with a few icons

00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:55.969
near the top right corner.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:00:56.969
Most of the photo editing I do is making thumbnails
using Photoshop on an iMac, so I wanted to

00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:57.969
see if I could make these photos pop and look
great.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:00.999
Testing the Machine Learning auto-enhance
feature, it really made the AirPods pop, and

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:04.400
it was surprising how well it does, although
it’s not perfect.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.030
You can easily zoom in on the photo by double-tapping
the screen.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.370
With the repair tool, you can easily get rid
of blemishes, and if it doesn’t work out

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.480
well, you can use the Apple Pencil’s new
double-tap feature to undo, and redo.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.480
Let’s make the repair selection a bit smaller
so we can perfectly get rid of this black

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.480
spot.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.090
The repair tool works amazingly well and it’s
incredible how quickly you can touch up photos.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.780
We can easily turn these 2nd gen AirPods into
regular AirPods, but let’s not.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.100
Within the crop menu, there’s a bunch of
options like instantly rotating the photo,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:41.840
flipping it vertically and horizontally, easily
adjusting the aspect ratio, finely adjusting

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.270
the level of the horizon, and warping the
perspective as well.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:47.670
Everything works so smoothly.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.350
We even tested the ML crop and it seemed to
work great, especially when we chose the 16:9

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.350
preset.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.660
They were perfectly centered.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:00.920
Now to the editing itself, there are tons
of options, and most of them have a ML button,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.560
and it’s usually very subtle in the changes
it makes, like only bringing up the saturation

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:06.850
by 9%.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.209
The lightness menu has every option you’ll
need, and after editing, you can disable each

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:14.030
adjustment menu you changed to see the difference.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.950
There are color balance wheels that let you
adjust the colors and brightness of Shadows,

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.760
mid tones, and highlights separately, which
is great for pros who know how to use it.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.020
There’s an awesome selective color menu
that allows you to select a certain color

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.769
and adjust the colors separate from the rest
of the photo.

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:31.920
See how I’m able to make this bokeh pop?

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.569
It’s really useful!

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:38.050
There’s levels, curves and a replace color
menu that can be extremely useful, i’ll

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.709
show you how later in the video.

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:44.290
Below that is a few filter menus that you
can adjust, and finally, sharpen, which is

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:47.439
great for editing RAW photos and some sharpness
back in.

00:02:47.439 --> 00:02:50.920
When you’re done, hit share, and you have
a couple of options.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.010
Modify original, save copy to photos, or export.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.400
Within export, you have a few formats to choose
from, and you can easily adjust the quality

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.060
to get your desired file size to where you
want it.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.709
Hit next, and you get Apple’s standard share
menu, so you can easily Airdrop it and save

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:07.709
it.

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:11.879
Here’s a RAW photo we used for a recent
iPad comparison, it was shot quite dark so

00:03:11.879 --> 00:03:12.879
let’s fix it.

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:17.650
We used the ML crop and it perfectly centered
the photo, again, really impressive!

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:22.430
After a few tweaks, we looked at the image
before and after, and the results are great!

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.239
Similar to what I was able to achieve using
Photoshop for Mac.

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:30.439
Now here’s a very difficult one, simply
because the face of the iPad Mini is dark

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:31.439
and orange.

00:03:31.439 --> 00:03:34.079
Let’s fix that using the replace color tool.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:38.060
Just grab the dropper, place it over the color
you want to replace.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.639
Then grab the other dropper and choose white.

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:46.110
It didn’t turn out so well so I tapped on
the color box and changed it to white.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.739
There you go, it looks great!

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:50.529
Just check out the before and after!

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:54.540
Here’s a photo I took using the iPad Pro.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.709
I wanted to see how much of these clouds you
can remove using the repair tool, and It’s

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:00.379
shocking how well it worked!

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:02.690
Look how easy it is to remove these rocks.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:07.739
I also used the color wheels menu to bring
up the saturation of the sky without affecting

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:08.739
everything else.

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:13.200
I also noticed that you can easily swipe the
whole menu to the left side if it’s more

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.610
comfortable for you.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.840
The repair tool works great for facial blemishes
as well.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.220
I’d personally never do this on my own face,
but you can see just how easily you can touch

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.280
up skin.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.389
Using selective color and adjust hues, you
can easily transform a wallpaper into something

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:29.590
totally unique.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.849
Now here’s where selective color really
comes in handy.

00:04:32.849 --> 00:04:36.180
See how the window is totally blue and it
ruins the whole photo?

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.180
Watch this.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:42.500
After brightening up the photo, I can go into
the selective color menu, choose blues, and

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.669
turn down the saturation.

00:04:44.669 --> 00:04:45.960
Look at that difference.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:50.550
Once finished, you can tap revert, and then
double-tap the Apple Pencil 2 to see the before

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:51.800
and after.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.530
This photo has been totally transformed!

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.310
Overall, I’m extremely impressed with Pixelmator
Photo for iPad.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.479
It’s very-well optimized and there was no
lag or any issues with exporting.

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:05.610
It works incredibly well with the Apple Pencil
2 thanks to the double-tap feature, and it

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:10.250
should work great with the new 2019 iPads
once they fix the compatibility issue.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.410
If you enjoyed this video, click that subscribe
button and check out our ultimate comparison

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.930
guide to choosing an iPad in 2019.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:22.449
Thanks for watching and we’ll see you in
the next one!

